# The change-narration rule catches a change described from the past,
# and the roadmap rule catches a claim promised for later. This rule
# catches a claim dated to the present. Bare `currently` is too common
# as runtime state (`the currently selected row`, `whether the item is
# currently available`), so it counts only before a word that names
# support or a limit, or after a negated or modal auxiliary. `TODO`
# stays out, because a code comment uses it as a marker.
#
# `at the moment` counts only at the end of a clause, because before a
# clause it means `when` (`spends its tokens at the moment it is
# called`). Its guard `moment!( )` takes any token but whitespace right
# after the phrase, which covers punctuation and the end of a list
# item.
expr main [(currently [supports, support, only, unsupported, not, no, limited, lacks, lack, requires, require, returns, accepts, uses, defaults, ignores, handles, allows, disallows, broken, missing, unimplemented, experimental, deprecated]), ([does, doesn't, do, don't, cannot, can't, will, won't] currently), (not currently), (at the moment!( )), (right now), (for now), (at present), (as of now), (at this time), (as of today), (at the time of writing), (as of this writing), (in the current version), (in the current release), (in the latest version), (in the latest release), (in this version), (in this release), nowadays, (these days), lately, (until recently)]
let message "Time-sensitive wording. Durable prose describes the system as it is, and git history records what changed."
let description "Flags phrases that date a claim to the current version."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Available reports whether the item is currently available."
allows "Cursor returns the currently selected row."
allows "The parser supports tables."
allows "TODO: handle the empty case."
allows "Reserve spends its tokens at the moment it is called."
